SUMMARY:Full Moon · Sydney Harbour Bridge from Barangaroo Reserve · Super
 moon on the arch (self guided)
LOCATION:Wulugul Walk\, Barangaroo Reserve\, Barangaroo NSW 2000
GEO:-33.85773;151.20014
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=-33.85773,151.20014
DESCRIPTION:WHAT: The January supermoon\, the closest full moon of 2027\, r
 ising behind the Harbour Bridge and climbing onto the top of the arch\, se
 en across the harbour from the Barangaroo foreshore. Moonrise is at sunset
 \, so the alignment lands in early blue hour. Self guided\, no booking.\nT
 arget: moon centred on the top of the arch.\n\nWHEN (Sydney time)\nFriday 
 22 January 2027  (work day)\nFull moon: Fri 22 Jan 23:17. Moon 100% lit at
  the alignment.\nSupermoon: about 357\,600 km centre to centre at full moo
 n\, the closest full moon of 2027.\nSunset 20:06 · civil twilight ends 20
 :34\nMoonrise 20:05 at azimuth 63° ENE (sea level horizon).\nALIGNMENT 20
 :44: moon altitude 6.4°\, azimuth 57.6°.\nArrive by 19:59. The moon move
 s about one diameter every 2 to 3 minutes\, so shoot from 10 minutes befor
 e to 10 minutes after.\n\nMOON TRACK\n20:14  altitude  1.4°\, azimuth  61
 .8°\n20:19  altitude  2.2°\, azimuth  61.1°\n20:24  altitude  3.0°\, a
 zimuth  60.4°\n20:29  altitude  3.9°\, azimuth  59.7°\n20:34  altitude 
  4.7°\, azimuth  59.0°\n20:39  altitude  5.6°\, azimuth  58.3°\n20:44 
  altitude  6.4°\, azimuth  57.6°  << alignment\n20:49  altitude  7.2°\,
  azimuth  56.9°\n20:54  altitude  8.1°\, azimuth  56.1°\n20:59  altitud
 e  8.9°\, azimuth  55.4°\n\nCAMERA POSITION\nExact alignment pin: https:
 //www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=-33.85773\,151.20014\nNamed spot
 : Wulugul Walk\, Barangaroo Reserve\, Barangaroo NSW 2000 (5 m from the ex
 act pin). https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=-33.85775\,151.2
 0010\nSubject pin (Sydney Harbour Bridge): https://www.google.com/maps/sea
 rch/?api=1&query=-33.85212\,151.21078\nFrom the exact pin\, Sydney Harbour
  Bridge is 1163 m away on bearing 57.6°.\nFrom the named spot it is on be
 aring 57.6°\, so there the moon passes 0.0° left of the target. Walk tow
 ard the exact pin to close the gap.\nStraight line from Neutral Bay: about
  3 km.\n\nTIDES (BOM prediction\, Sydney (Fort Denison))\nFort Denison is 
 about 2 km away in the same harbour\, so times are close. The promenade si
 ts above the water\, so tide only changes the foreground.\nHigh water Fri 
 21:30  1.46 m\nAt the alignment (20:44): about 1.41 m and rising.\nPredict
 ions exclude swell\, wind setup and wave run up. Check BOM coastal waters 
 and swell forecasts on the day.\nSource table: https://www.bom.gov.au/ntc/
 IDO59001/IDO59001_2027_NSW_TP007.pdf\n\nFRAMING (Sony A1)\nSydney Harbour 
 Bridge spans about 6.6° tall from here. The moon is 0.52° wide\, about 1
 1 m across at this distance.\nLandmark filling 60% of the frame height: FE
  70-200mm F2.8 GM OSS II at about 125 mm.\nLongest reach in your kit: FE 7
 0-200mm F2.8 GM OSS II with the 2x teleconverter at 400 mm (f/5.6). The mo
 on fills about 15% of the frame height.\nLocked tripod\, OSS off\, 2 s tim
 er or remote. Stills at 1/125 s or faster at 400 mm.\nStart: ISO 200 to 40
 0\, f/8\, expose for the moon. After civil twilight\, bracket 3 frames at 
 2 EV and blend. For video\, expose for the moon and let the sky go deep bl
 ue.\n\nFIELD NOTES\n- Wulugul Walk is the public harbour promenade along B
 arangaroo Reserve. Barangaroo ferry wharf and Barangaroo Metro station are
  a short walk\, so skip city parking.\n- Backup spot\, same night: Illoura
  Reserve\, Balmain East. The moon reaches arch height at about 20:33\, abo
 ut 2.5° left of the top\, on the arch's left shoulder. A paid photo group
  is listed at Balmain East that night\, so expect company there.\n- If the
  22nd is cloudy\, the 21st and 23rd do not align from here. Rework the pin
  in TPE first.\n- Video: the moon climbs its own diameter in about 2 minut
 es. Start recording at moonrise and run one continuous clip past the align
 ment. Set exposure\, focus and white balance manually so the clip does not
  pump.\n- Supermoon nights draw crowds on the harbour. Arrive early to cla
 im a spot on the rail.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE YOU GO\nTPE Web (camera pin\, subj
 ect pin\, alignment time): https://app.photoephemeris.com/?ll=-33.85773\,1
 51.20014&center=-33.85773\,151.20014&z=16&dt=2027-01-22T09%3A44%3A00.000Z&
 sll=-33.85212\,151.21078&fovb=57.6&fovhfov=16.32&fovvfov=10.95&fovd=1163&f
 ovo=l&fovov=1\nIn TPE\, set the secondary height to about 134 m above grou
 nd to check the geodetics line.\nPhotoPills: Planner > red pin on the came
 ra pin\, black pin on the subject > Find > Moon\, elevation 6.4° > confir
 m the date. Use the AR view on site.\nHeights are approximate. Fine tune b
 y a few metres on the day.\n\nSOURCES\nMoon phases: US Naval Observatory 2
 027 table (aa.usno.navy.mil).\nMoon and sun positions: computed with Skyfi
 eld and JPL DE421\, with standard refraction.\nCoordinates: OpenStreetMap.
  Ground heights: SRTM 30 m via OpenTopoData.\nTide predictions: Bureau of 
 Meteorology 2027 tide tables\, Copyright Commonwealth of Australia 2026. B
 ased on Bureau of Meteorology information that has been modified. The Bure
 au does not necessarily support or endorse\, or have any connection with\,
  this product\, and gives no warranty about the information. Full terms: p
 age 4 of the source PDF.
CATEGORIES:Astrophotography
